  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nEarly Thanksgiving Snow Shifting South\n\nThe ingredients for what is shaping up to be an interesting \nThanksgiving winter storm are coming together across the Midwest. An \nexpansive mid to upper level moisture plume ahead of a northern \nstream low currently diving down the Missouri River is currently providing\nus with a fetch of high clouds (and light snow over the Siouxland).\nThe low works into the lower Missouri River Valley over the next\n18-24 hours and slows as a southern stream shortwave counterpart\nrotates cyclonically around the mean longwave trough base, across\nthe northern Gulf, and eventually up the Eastern Seaboard for Thursday.\nA north-to-south inverted near-surface trough sets up within a region\nof potent kinematic and isentropic ascent for Thursday, potentially\nyielding an impressive early season snowstorm focused in western Illinois.\n\nThe base of this inverted trough pivots through the region for \ntonight into Thursday morning. Its passage looks to be transient and \nof questionable moisture content, but there still exists a roughly 5-\n10% probability for snow--mainly a dusting at best--along and south \nof Highway 18 in IA/WI. These probabilities ramp up steadily as one \ntravels south of Dubuque with model histograms over east-central \nIowa and south showing a bimodal distribution indicative of the \nuncertainties in the potent band placement.\n\nFocusing on the local area, with model guidance trending drier and \nfarther south, have scaled back the messaging on any impacts from \nsnow. Those with travel plans further south during this timeframe \nshould still pay attention to this event.\n\nWeekend Snow/Rain Potential\n\nShortwave ridging moves over the region for Friday with zonal flow \nfor Saturday. A weak front passes through the Upper Midwest Friday \nnight into Saturday morning bringing about a low chance (10-20%) for \nsnow or rain showers with temperatures around the freezing mark. \nEnsemble and deterministic models support a shortwave coming down \nSunday, however there are differences in the strength of this wave \nand attendant moisture. Of the available LREF guidance at that \nrange, about 20-40% of the members have accumulating precipitation \nover the local area with this Sunday system.\n\nTemperature Trends through the Weekend\n\nAn elongated Great Lakes high pressure system remains the dominant \nsurface feature in play through Friday, with a mean north to \nnortheasterly fetch keeping temperatures near seasonal norms. That \nbeing said, the ridge axis pivots cyclonically over the region for \nthe end of the week as our Thanksgiving system lifts up the Eastern \nSeaboard. Lower tropospheric warm air advection takes place Thursday \nas the warm sector responsible for the snow to the south pivots \naround the aforementioned upper level low; however, a continued \nnortherly surface fetch tempers the degree of warming we see with \nthis modified maritime tropical airmass and highs only look to warm \na few degrees between today and Thursday. The ridge orients \nmeridionally for late Friday and the surface flow turns more \nsouthwesterly, ushering in a weak ribbon of positive theta-e \nadvection late Friday into Saturday. Increasing insolation for \nFriday should push temperatures into the mid-40s with the warmest \nday on Saturday as the clearer skies and warmer boundary-layer \ntemperatures align to produce highs in the mid-40s to near 50.",
